Article 17
(1) Prevention of violence against women is carried out in the following fields:
a. education;
b. health;
c. religion;
d. security;
e. judiciary;
f. public works and spatial planning;
g. public housing and settlement areas;
h. social affairs;
i. labor;
j. women's empowerment and child protection;
k. land affairs;
l. environment;
m. population administration and civil registration;
n. population control and family planning;
o. transportation;
p. communication and informatics;
q. cooperatives, small and medium enterprises;
r. youth and sports;
s. culture;
t. tourism;
u. trade and industry; and
v. other related fields.
(2) Prevention of violence against women as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out in the following forms:
a. developing media for communication, information, education and public campaigns through media;
b. developing educational materials and curriculum;
c. developing safe transportation systems and public spaces;
d. establishing early detection systems, security and integrated complaint services in industrial areas, companies, residential areas, educational environments, Islamic boarding schools and other public spaces;
e. providing consultation services for families, prospective brides and grooms, students, Islamic boarding school students, youth, university students and vulnerable groups;
f. encouraging behavioral change of parents and the community;
g. developing policies related to regencies/cities, villages, schools, Islamic boarding schools, and universities that are safe and friendly for women and integrated with regional spatial planning policies;
h. forming and developing cadres, communities, and groups from among youth, students, Islamic boarding school students, university students, journalists, social media influencers, educators, and religious leaders for prevention;
i. conducting education and advocacy to owners, managers and/or users of social media;
j. developing protection systems and special support for vulnerable women groups;
k. conducting awareness-raising for perpetrators;
l. conducting cooperation between those responsible for prevention of violence against women; and
m. conducting socialization of laws and regulations.
(3) The fields and forms of prevention of violence against women as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) shall be the responsibility of Regional Apparatus that oversee related affairs.

REGENT OF MAMUJU
WEST SULAWESI PROVINCE
REGIONAL REGULATION OF MAMUJU REGENCY
NUMBER 9 YEAR 2023
CONCERNING
EMPOWERMENT AND PROTECTION OF WOMEN
BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD
THE REGENT OF MAMUJU,

Considering : a. that the fulfillment of constitutional rights and protection of women's human rights regarding empowerment and protection of women is one of the values contained in Pancasila and the 1945 Constitution;
b. that women play a very important role in the process of continuation and creation of quality generations, thus requiring a sense of security, obtaining guarantees of protection from acts of violence and discrimination, and need to be empowered so that they can actualize their potential optimally;
c. that based on Law Number 23 Year 2014 concerning Regional Government as mentioned in the Appendix that the division of government affairs in the field of women's empowerment with one of the sub-affairs is the protection of women;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning Empowerment and Protection of Women;

In View of : 1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 29 Year 1959 concerning the Establishment of Level II Regions in Sulawesi (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1959 Number 74, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 1822);
3. Law Number 23 Year 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as amended several times most recently by Law Number 6 Year 2023 concerning Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 2 Year 2022 concerning Job Creation into Law (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2023 Number 41, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6856);
4. Law Number 12 Year 2022 concerning Criminal Acts of Sexual Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2022 Number 120, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6792);

With the Mutual Approval of THE REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF MAMUJU REGENCY and

Article 16
(1) Prevention of violence against women is intended to:
a. create a safe environment for women; and
b. reduce vulnerability to violence, exploitation and discrimination against vulnerable groups of women.
(2) Vulnerable groups of women as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b are as follows:
a. women in disaster and social conflict situations;
b. women with disabilities;
c. women with HIV/AIDS;
d. women migrant workers;
e. girls;
f. women heads of household;
g. women domestic workers;
h. home-based women workers;
i. elderly women;
j. informal women workers;
k. women in situations of intolerance; and
l. other vulnerable groups of women.

Article 23
(1) Services are implemented to fulfill the rights of women victims of violence and are carried out with the following principles:
a. respect for human rights; b. non-discrimination;
c. confidentiality;
d. empathy;
e. inclusion;
f. empowerment;
g. victim-based decisions;
h. honesty;
i. professionalism;
j. integration; and
k. sustainability.
(2) Services to women victims of violence must consider special conditions and needs based on the vulnerabilities experienced by women as referred to in Article 20

Article 24
(1) The forms of services as referred to in Article 23 include:
a. complaint services;
b. health services;
c. social rehabilitation services;
d. legal aid and law enforcement services; and
e. repatriation and social reintegration services.
(2) Services for women victims of violence shall be implemented promptly, free of charge, comprehensively, with quality and integrated with services provided by government agencies, communities, educational institutions, religious institutions, professional institutions, Islamic boarding schools, and the business sector through an integrated service system.

CHAPTER VII
COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION
Article 39
(1) The community participates in the implementation of empowerment and protection of women.
(2) Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be in the form of:
a. providing education regarding women's rights;
b. providing skills training to support the community's economy;
c. establishing social community institutions to assist victims of violence;
d. providing information and/or reporting violence against women to law enforcement or the authorities; and/or
e. participating in handling acts of violence against women.

Article 15
Prevention of violence against women is the responsibility of:
a. Regional Government;
b. educational institutions;
c. religious institutions;
d. health service provider institutions;
e. professional institutions;
f. business sector;
g. service institutions;
h. Islamic boarding schools;
i. youth organizations;
j. legal aid organizations;
k. communities; and
l. media.

Article 20
(1) In the implementation of protection services for women, the Regional Government establishes P2TP2A as a service network for women victims of violence.
(2) The establishment of P2TP2A is intended so that women victims of violence obtain comprehensive, integrated, quality, inclusive and sustainable services.
(3) P2TP2A as referred to in paragraph (1) has the following duties:
 a. provide services to women victims of violence in accordance with standard operating procedures;
 b. coordinate prevention, services and referrals between government-owned service institutions, community-owned service institutions, professional organizations, law enforcement institutions and other related institutions;
 c. conduct data collection on case services;
 d. provide various information needed by victims, victims' families and the community;
 e. conduct prevention of recurrence of violence, exploitation and discrimination against women;
 f. conduct monitoring and evaluation of victim services; and
 g. provide capacity strengthening, guidance and assistance related to victim services.

Article 27
(1) Social rehabilitation services as referred to in Article 24 paragraph (1) letter c shall be implemented with the following activities:
a. counseling;
b. assistance;
c. linguists and/or interpreters;
d. spiritual guidance;
e. shelters and/or safe houses;
f. temporary shelter;
g. strengthening based on family and/or group support;
h. mobility support for victims with disabilities;
i. provision of special social assistance for victims; and
j. other forms of social rehabilitation services needed by victims.
(2) Social rehabilitation services as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a, letter b, letter c, letter d, letter f, letter g, letter h, and letter i shall be the responsibility of Regional Apparatus in charge of women's empowerment and child protection affairs.
(3) Social rehabilitation services as referred to in paragraph (1) letter e and letter j shall be the responsibility of Regional Apparatus in charge of social affairs and women's empowerment and child protection affairs.
(4) Further provisions regarding social rehabilitation services shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

CHAPTER III
WOMEN'S RIGHTS
Article 3
(1) Every woman has the right to:
a. live and maintain life and improve her standard of living;
b. fulfill her basic needs to grow and develop properly, have a family and continue offspring;
c. develop her personality, to obtain education, and improve her quality of life in order to become a human being who is faithful, pious, responsible, of noble character, happy, and prosperous in accordance with human rights;
d. obtain justice, security, and freedom to express opinions without discrimination;
e. be involved in every stage of the development process;
f. be free from slavery or enslavement;
g. be free from threats;
h. obtain protection of her person, family, honor, dignity and property rights;
i. obtain welfare and a decent life;
j. participate in the political, economic, social and cultural fields;
k. perform legal acts; and
l. be free to choose a partner in her marriage.
(2) Every wife during the marriage bond has the same rights and responsibilities as her husband over all matters relating to her marital life, relations with her children, and ownership and management of joint property in accordance with applicable laws and regulations.
(3) After the dissolution of marriage, a woman has rights and responsibilities with her former husband over all matters relating to children with due regard to the best interests of the child.

Article 25
(1) Complaint services as referred to in Article 24 paragraph (1) letter a consist of:
a. complaint reception;
b. special complaints;
c. provision of information;
d. outreach;
e. crisis or emergency services;
f. assessment; and
g. other forms of complaint services needed by victims.
(2) Complaint services as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out by trained officers in a special room that is safe and comfortable.
(3) Further provisions regarding complaint services shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 26
(1) Health services as referred to in Article 24 paragraph (1) letter b shall be implemented with the following activities:
a. physical and mental health examination and treatment;
b. psychological examination and recovery;
c. pregnancy, childbirth, and reproductive health services;
d. medical resume and psychological examination results;
e. visum et repertum and visum et psikiatrikum;
f. Deoxyribo Nucleic Acid test; and
g. other forms of health services needed by victims.
(2) Health services for female victims shall be carried out by trained officers in a special room that is safe and comfortable.
(3) Health services as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out through a special mechanism of integrated services based on hospitals and shall be the responsibility of Regional Apparatus that administers health affairs.
(4) Further provisions regarding health services shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 29
(1) Repatriation and social reintegration services as referred to in Article 24 paragraph (1) letter e shall be implemented with the following activities:
 a. repatriation and/or retrieval of victims;
 b. provision of population documents or personal identity;
 c. alternative family;
 d. scholarships and educational support facilities;
 e. non-formal education;
 f. skills training and economic enterprise;
 g. financial assistance and business capital;
 h. economic enterprise assistance;
 i. housing assistance;
 j. food and/or nutrition assistance for victims and their families;
 k. provision of social protection programs and poverty alleviation programs;
 l. other forms of repatriation and social reintegration services needed by victims.
(2) Repatriation and social reintegration services as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be provided to victims, families and persons whose lives depend on victims with the aim that victims have the readiness and ability to live life in society.
(3) Repatriation and social reintegration services as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be conducted in an integrated manner with the Integrated Referral Service System for social protection and poverty alleviation and the Child Social Welfare Center.
(4) Repatriation and social reintegration services as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be the responsibility of Regional Apparatus in charge of:
 a. social affairs;
 b. population administration and civil registration affairs;
 c. labor affairs;
 d. small and medium enterprise cooperative affairs;
 e. education affairs;
 f. health affairs;
 g. public works and spatial planning affairs;
 h. land affairs, women's empowerment and child protection affairs; and
 i. other related affairs.
(5) Further provisions regarding repatriation and social reintegration services shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

